    id: "camera-interface", title: "Camera, selection and observation modes", summary: "How to inspect without changing the simulated animal.",
    html: `<p>Map view supports orbit, zoom, selection and camera locking. Ground constraints prevent the camera or orbit target from passing through rendered terrain. Locking preserves the viewing offset while following an organism.</p>
      <p>The Selected Organism panel separates overview, social state, commitment and overlays. Main Laboratory opens a full-width workspace; Mini Laboratory keeps concise live summaries. Entity focus shows only what the selected organism currently knows, while Body only removes world context for visual inspection. Neither mode alters decisions.</p>
      <p>Entity constellations use two selection disclosures rather than exchanging near, medium and far surfaces at zoom thresholds. In an unselected strategic view, animals may receive compact public rails under the centre-weighted budget. Selecting a living animal suppresses every other animal rail and standalone nameplate, leaving one integrated observer instrument containing the same identity header, full-width health, plain-language immediate concern and forecast effect, metabolic reserves and fuel-and-performance rows. The selected instrument and its transparent thought-and-forecast attachment bays share one root, outer border, collision footprint, scale and owner tether. Each wheel event samples camera-to-animal distance and stores a bounded presentation scale for the complete active root; it never substitutes a ground-distance measurement. Camera orbit, pitch and map movement update viewport intersection but do not revise that stored scale, so a changing ground angle cannot unexpectedly resize a panel. Cinema uses the same exclusive admission rule with its author-selected camera-primary animal and does not write to observer selection; subjectless world shots show no entity panel. After deselection, the centre-weighted budget, interaction fanning and residual overlap suppression resume for public rails.</p>
      <p>At strategic altitude, individual meshes can become numbered aggregate markers. This is a performance presentation choice, not population compression.</p>`
